---
// The full accuracy record. Every figure is rendered from
// pace-sdk/results/accuracy.json at build time; websites/scripts/check_site_accuracy.py
// re-checks the built page against the artifact in CI, so a hand-typed number
// cannot survive here.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site, href } from '../../data/site';
import { measurements, decomposition, environment, fine } from '../../data/measurements';
import AccuracyLog from '../../components/charts/AccuracyLog.astro';
import PairedRuns from '../../components/charts/PairedRuns.astro';
import DecompositionFigure from '../../components/charts/DecompositionFigure.astro';

// "on-CPU time vs /proc/<tid>/schedstat (kernel cfs accounting)" -> the source.
function against(name: string): string {
  const cut = name.indexOf(' vs ');
  return cut === -1 ? name : name.slice(cut + 4);
}

const cfs = measurements.find((m) => m.short === 'on-CPU vs schedstat')!;
const perfRun = measurements.find((m) => m.short === 'on-CPU vs perf run time')!;
const perfWait = measurements.find((m) => m.short === 'off-CPU vs perf wait time')!;
const perfDelay = measurements.find((m) => m.short === 'preempted vs perf sch delay')!;
---

<Base
  title="Accuracy — PACE"
  description="Every cross-check PACE's harness makes, the source each one is compared against, the gate it must pass, and the method behind the numbers."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="breadcrumb"><a href={href()}>PACE</a> / <a href={href('research/')}>research</a> / accuracy</div>
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="section-label">Research · accuracy</div>
      <h1>What the harness measures, and what it is gated on</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The claim is that PACE's split of a thread's wall time is real rather than modelled. The way
        to test that is to measure the same window a second time, from a source that does not come
        from PACE, and compare. This page is the result of doing that, unedited: the figures that are
        strong, the one that is looser, and the reason it is looser.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        Every number below is rendered from
        {' '}<a href={`${site.repo}/blob/main/pace-sdk/results/accuracy.json`}>the committed
        artifact</a> when this site is built. None of it is typed into the page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The table</div>
      <h2>Every cross-check, with the gate it passes or misses</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Measured</th>
              <th>Compared against</th>
              <th>n</th>
              <th>Median</th>
              <th>p95</th>
              <th>Gate</th>
              <th>Verdict</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              measurements.map((m) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{m.short}</td>
                  <td class="dim" style="white-space:normal">{against(m.name)}</td>
                  <td class="dim">{m.n}</td>
                  <td>{fine(m.median)}</td>
                  <td>{fine(m.p95)}</td>
                  <td class="dim">{fine(m.threshold)}</td>
                  <td class={m.skipped ? 'dim' : m.passed ? 'ok' : 'bad'}>
                    {m.skipped ? 'reported' : m.passed ? 'pass' : 'fail'}
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
            <tr>
              <td>decomposition balance</td>
              <td class="dim" style="white-space:normal">every recorded sample</td>
              <td class="dim">{decomposition.samples.toLocaleString('en-US')}</td>
              <td>0.0000 %</td>
              <td>0.0000 %</td>
              <td class="dim">0.0100 %</td>
              <td class="ok">pass</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        The unit is relative error, as a percentage of the quantity being measured. "compared against"
        is the second source: the kernel's own accounting, an independently read clock, or perf's
        scheduling analysis.
      </p>
      <AccuracyLog />
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Reading the numbers</div>
      <h2>Median, p95 and gate are three different statements</h2>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-target"></i> Median</h3>
          <p>
            What a typical run does. The headline agreement with the kernel's own accounting is
            {' '}{fine(cfs.median)}, which is four orders of magnitude below the gate.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-chart-line-down"></i> p95</h3>
          <p>
            The tail. This is the column that tells you whether the good median is an average over a
            bimodal distribution, and it is reported because a median alone can hide exactly that.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-door"></i> Gate</h3>
          <p>
            The threshold the build fails on. It is deliberately looser than the measured figure, so
            that a regression shows up as a failure rather than as a quiet drift.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">Why the preempted row against perf is the loosest, and why that is not a defect in PACE</div>
        <p>
          The kernel's run-queue delay accumulates continuously, from the moment a thread becomes
          runnable to the moment it runs. <span class="mono">perf</span> can only measure the
          wakeup-to-running interval where it captured a <span class="mono">sched_wakeup</span>
          tracepoint, so its figure is the same quantity under a different instrumentation with a
          coverage gap. The two disagree at {fine(perfDelay.median)} median, and that number is
          labelled <em>reported, not gated</em> in the artifact for exactly this reason. The tighter
          cross-check on the same underlying phenomenon is the off-CPU row, where PACE and perf agree
          to {fine(perfWait.median)}.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-clock-afternoon"></i> On-CPU against perf's own run time</h3>
          <p>
            {fine(perfRun.median)} median, {fine(perfRun.p95)} at p95. This is the check that says
            PACE's on-CPU figure is not an artefact of agreeing with one particular kernel interface.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-git-compare"></i> Off-CPU: preempted plus blocked</h3>
          <p>
            {fine(perfWait.median)} median. Off-CPU is the sum of the two buckets PACE splits apart,
            so this is the check that the split sums to something perf can see independently.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The invariant</div>
      <h2>The arithmetic, checked on every sample rather than asserted</h2>
      <DecompositionFigure />
      <p>
        A violation count of zero over
        {' '}{decomposition.samples.toLocaleString('en-US')} samples is a stronger statement than a
        small percentage, and it is the one this project would treat as a defect if it ever changed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">The distribution</div>
      <h2>Every run, not just the summary statistic</h2>
      <PairedRuns />
      <p>
        A median is a compression of thirty runs into one number. The figure above is the thirty
        runs, so you can see the spread rather than trust the summary. The two series are the two
        regimes the harness exercises: a window where the thread stays runnable, and a window where
        it is off-CPU for almost all of it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-label">Method and reproducibility</div>
      <h2>How the numbers were produced, and what would invalidate them</h2>
      <div class="grid-2">
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-list-numbers"></i> The protocol</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>N &ge; 30 independent runs per configuration, median and p95 reported rather than means.</li>
            <li>Core pinning through <span class="mono">taskset</span> so a migration cannot be mistaken for a preemption.</li>
            <li>The governor and the turbo state recorded in the artifact, so a result taken at a different frequency is identifiable as such.</li>
            <li>The rules from the <em>Rigorous Benchmarking in C++</em> paper applied to the harness itself, not only to the product.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="panel">
          <h3><i class="ph ph-flask"></i> What would invalidate these figures</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>A different kernel scheduler. These are measurements of this kernel's accounting as much as of PACE, and a scheduler change moves both sides.</li>
            <li>A container or VM where <span class="mono">/proc/&lt;tid&gt;/schedstat</span> is not the host's. The benchmark here ran outside one.</li>
            <li>A machine whose timer resolution differs materially from {environment.clk_tck} Hz, which is what sets the floor on the tick-granularity comparison.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-title">The environment these ran in</div>
        <p>
          {environment.cpu_model}, {environment.cpu_count} hardware threads,
          {' '}<span class="mono">{environment.uname}</span>, governor
          {' '}<span class="mono">{environment.governor}</span>,
          {' '}<span class="mono">perf_event_paranoid={environment.kernel_perf_event_paranoid}</span>,
          recorded {environment.timestamp_utc}. One machine is one machine; the harness is in the
          repository so that a second machine is a command rather than an argument.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn" href={href('research/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-arrow-left"></i>
          Back to the research index
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={href('research/prior-art/')}>
          <i class="ph ph-scales"></i>
          Prior art
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
